# The ledger of `unicode/encodings`: the legacy encodings of the WHATWG
# Encoding Standard (the Living Standard of 19 October 2026, its indexes dated
# 2024-09-18) read and written, and the encoding of bytes nobody declared
# guessed. One block per capability, and what Heroes answers to each.
#
# The census, by the author's decision of 2026-11-17, is wider than the three
# libraries of README § A8, since Go's and Rust's standard libraries hold no
# legacy encoding: `go` is Go 1.27's `unicode/utf16` with
# `golang.org/x/text` v0.38.0's `encoding` packages (`go doc -all`, from the
# module cache); `rust` is Rust 1.100's `String` and `char` with the crate
# `encoding_rs` 0.8.42, the yardstick (its `cargo doc`, built offline from the
# cargo registry; no source opened); `python` is Python 3.15.2's `codecs`,
# `encodings` and the codecs it lists (`dir()`); `java` is Java SE 25's
# `java.nio.charset` with the `jdk.charsets` module, and `dotnet` is .NET's
# `System.Text.Encoding` with `System.Text.Encoding.CodePages`, both read from
# their documentation on the web on 2026-11-17, since neither runtime is on
# this machine (the census's one unrun list).

## Names and labels

capability label/lookup  the encoding a label names, as a browser reads a `charset`
  heroes  unicode/encodings.name_of
  go      htmlindex.Get, ianaindex.Index.Encoding, ianaindex.MIME, ianaindex.IANA, ianaindex.MIB
  rust    Encoding::for_label, Encoding::for_label_no_replacement
  python  codecs.lookup, encodings.search_function, encodings.normalize_encoding, encodings.aliases.aliases, encodings.CodecRegistryError, LookupError
  java    Charset.forName, Charset.isSupported, IllegalCharsetNameException, UnsupportedCharsetException
  dotnet  Encoding.GetEncoding, CodePagesEncodingProvider.GetEncoding, EncodingProvider.GetEncoding, CodePagesEncodingProvider.Instance
  note    a label is matched as the standard says, ASCII white space taken off and ASCII case ignored, and nothing else is: `latin1`, `iso-8859-1` and `ascii` name windows-1252, `utf-16` names UTF-16LE, `gb2312` GBK, ISO-2022-KR, ISO-2022-CN and HZ `replacement`. Python's `lookup` normalizes `-` and `_` and reads `latin_1` as true Latin-1, Java and .NET read their own aliases; a label the standard does not read fails `not_found` (Go answers an error, Rust None, Python LookupError). 228 labels, `encodings.json` of the standard

