## Decoding

capability decode/replacing  bytes read as text in an encoding, a byte order mark first, what cannot be read replaced by U+FFFD
  heroes  unicode/encodings.decode
  go      encoding.Decoder.Bytes, encoding.Decoder.String, charmap.Charmap.DecodeByte, charmap.Charmap.NewDecoder, encoding.Encoding.NewDecoder
  rust    Encoding::decode, Encoding::decode_with_bom_removal
  python  bytes.decode, codecs.decode, codecs.getdecoder, codecs.replace_errors, codecs.Codec.decode
  java    Charset.decode, CodingErrorAction.REPLACE, CharsetDecoder.replaceWith, CharsetDecoder.replacement, CharsetDecoder.onMalformedInput, CharsetDecoder.onUnmappableCharacter, CharsetDecoder.malformedInputAction, CharsetDecoder.unmappableCharacterAction
  dotnet  Encoding.GetString, Encoding.GetChars, Encoding.TryGetChars, Encoding.DecoderFallback, DecoderFallback.ReplacementFallback, DecoderReplacementFallback, DecoderReplacementFallback.DefaultString
  note    the standard's decode: a byte order mark overrules the label, each wrong sequence is one U+FFFD as the standard's decoders say. Differential of 2026-11-17 against encoding_rs 0.8.42 (built offline, its answers asked through a program of the round's scratch folder): every byte of every single-byte encoding, every pair from a lead 0x80 to 0xFF of the six double-byte encodings, every JIS X 0212 triple, 40,000 random four-byte gb18030 sequences and the ranges' edges, every code point of every index written both ways, every BMP code point and 3,000 others written in gb18030, and 3,500 ISO-2022-JP pairs: 628,034 cases, all equal; and twice 328,800 random cases (bytes read whole, without a mark, strict, and in random pieces; texts written strict, with references and in pieces) equal but for the shape `decode/valid-prefix` names. Python's codecs, where they are the standard's, agree on the single-byte indexes but for the bytes 0x80 to 0x9F that Windows leaves undefined (the standard reads them as the C1 controls, Python fails) and KOI8-U's 0xAE and 0xBE; `cp932` and `cp949` agree on every pointer of index jis0208 and EUC-KR; `gb18030` differs on 20 pointers, `big5hkscs` on 203, `euc_jp` and `iso2022_jp` on 463, `shift_jis` on 851

capability decode/without-bom  bytes read in the encoding named, a byte order mark read as a character
  heroes  unicode/encodings.decode_without_bom
  go      unicode.IgnoreBOM
  rust    Encoding::decode_without_bom_handling
  python  -
  java    -
  dotnet  -

capability decode/strict  bytes read in an encoding, failing at the first that cannot be read
  heroes  unicode/encodings.decode_strict
  go      encoding.Decoder.Bytes
  rust    Encoding::decode_without_bom_handling_and_without_replacement
  python  codecs.strict_errors, UnicodeDecodeError
  java    CodingErrorAction.REPORT, CharsetDecoder.decode, CharacterCodingException, MalformedInputException, UnmappableCharacterException, CoderResult.isError, CoderResult.isMalformed, CoderResult.isUnmappable, CoderResult.throwException
  dotnet  DecoderExceptionFallback, DecoderFallback.ExceptionFallback, DecoderFallbackException
  note    the standard's "fatal" mode; no byte order mark is looked for, as encoding_rs's `_without_replacement` decoder does; the web-platform-tests' vectors, fetched 2026-11-17 into the round's scratch folder, answer as they say: the 34 of `encoding/textdecoder-fatal.any.js` each fail, and the 5 of `textdecoder-utf16-surrogates.any.js` read as they expect replacing and fail strict

capability decode/valid-prefix  how many bytes at the start read without an error, where the first error is
  heroes  unicode/encodings.valid_prefix_len
  go      -
  rust    Encoding::utf8_valid_up_to, Encoding::ascii_valid_up_to, Encoding::iso_2022_jp_ascii_valid_up_to
  python  UnicodeDecodeError.start, UnicodeDecodeError.end
  java    CoderResult.length, CoderResult.malformedForLength, CoderResult.unmappableForLength, MalformedInputException.getInputLength
  dotnet  DecoderFallbackException.Index, DecoderFallbackException.BytesUnknown
  note    the index where the first wrong sequence begins, encoding_rs's index on every case of the differential but one shape: where ISO-2022-JP holds two escape sequences in a row, encoding_rs names the first and this the second, where the bytes stop reading (11 of 1,500 random ISO-2022-JP cases)

capability decode/stream  bytes read as text a piece at a time, a character cut between two pieces kept whole
  heroes  unicode/encodings.decoder, unicode/encodings.decoder_without_bom, unicode/encodings.decode_chunk, unicode/encodings.decode_chunk_strict
  go      encoding.Decoder.Reader, transform.Transformer
  rust    Encoding::new_decoder, Encoding::new_decoder_with_bom_removal, Encoding::new_decoder_without_bom_handling, Decoder::decode_to_string, Decoder::decode_to_string_without_replacement, Decoder::decode_to_str, Decoder::decode_to_str_without_replacement, Decoder::decode_to_utf8, Decoder::decode_to_utf8_without_replacement, Decoder::encoding, DecoderResult, CoderResult
  python  codecs.getincrementaldecoder, codecs.IncrementalDecoder, codecs.BufferedIncrementalDecoder, codecs.iterdecode
  java    Charset.newDecoder, CharsetDecoder.decode, CharsetDecoder.flush, CharsetDecoder.reset, CharsetDecoder.charset, CoderResult.UNDERFLOW, CoderResult.OVERFLOW, CoderResult.isUnderflow, CoderResult.isOverflow
  dotnet  Encoding.GetDecoder, Decoder.GetChars, Decoder.Convert, Decoder.Reset, Decoder.Fallback, Decoder.FallbackBuffer
  note    the pieces' texts joined are the whole's, held to encoding_rs's decoder over random cuts of 1,500 random inputs an encoding; a strict piece that fails leaves the decoder as it was

