capability legacy/outside-the-standard  an encoding the WHATWG standard does not name: EBCDIC, the DOS code pages, ISO-8859-9 and 11, the other Mac encodings, KOI8-T, HZ, ISO-2022-KR and CN, Johab, JIS X 0213, CESU-8
  heroes  refused: the standard's set is closed, a browser reading no other; ISO-2022-KR, ISO-2022-CN and HZ are the label `replacement`, which reads them as one U+FFFD so that no filter is fooled; ISO-8859-9 and 11 are windows-1254 and windows-874 by label, as the standard reads them
  go      charmap.CodePage037, charmap.CodePage1047, charmap.CodePage1140, charmap.CodePage437, charmap.CodePage850, charmap.CodePage852, charmap.CodePage855, charmap.CodePage858, charmap.CodePage860, charmap.CodePage862, charmap.CodePage863, charmap.CodePage865, charmap.ISO8859_9, simplifiedchinese.HZGB2312
  rust    -
  python  the codecs cp037, cp1006, cp1026, cp1125, cp1140, cp273, cp424, cp437, cp500, cp720, cp737, cp775, cp850, cp852, cp855, cp856, cp857, cp858, cp860, cp861, cp862, cp863, cp864, cp865, cp869, cp875, cp932, cp949, cp950, euc_jis_2004, euc_jisx0213, gb2312, hp_roman8, hz, iso2022_jp_1, iso2022_jp_2, iso2022_jp_2004, iso2022_jp_3, iso2022_jp_ext, iso2022_kr, iso8859_9, iso8859_11, johab, koi8_t, kz1048, mac_arabic, mac_croatian, mac_farsi, mac_greek, mac_iceland, mac_latin2, mac_romanian, mac_turkish, palmos, ptcp154, shift_jis_2004, shift_jisx0213, tis_620, big5, mbcs, oem
  java    every charset of `jdk.charsets` outside the standard's list: the IBM and EBCDIC sets, x-MacArabic and the other Mac sets, ISO-2022-CN, ISO-2022-KR, ISO-2022-JP-2, x-Johab, x-SJIS_0213, x-EUC-TW, JIS_X0201, CESU-8, x-ISCII91, and the rest of the page
  dotnet  the code pages of `CodePagesEncodingProvider` outside the standard's list
  note    Python's `cp932`, `cp949` and `big5hkscs` are the nearest of its codecs to the standard's Shift_JIS, EUC-KR and Big5, which read the vendors' extensions as browsers do; the differences are measured in the tests' comments

## Guessing an encoding

capability detect/likeliest  the encoding of bytes whose encoding nobody declared, with how sure the guess is
  heroes  unicode/encodings.detect
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    CharsetDecoder.isAutoDetecting, CharsetDecoder.isCharsetDetected, CharsetDecoder.detectedCharset, x-JISAutoDetect
  dotnet  -
  note    no standard library guesses; PyPI's `charset-normalizer`, the seventh most downloaded project, is the program this answers to (the author's decision of 2026-11-17); measured 2026-11-17 on 3,626 samples of 40, 300 and 3,000 characters cut from macOS 27's localized strings of 31 languages (the frameworks', held out from the applications' strings the alphabets were measured on), each written by Python's codecs in an encoding its language is written in, a guess counted right where it reads the bytes as the text that made them: this package 3,537 right (97.5 %: 94.3 % at 40 characters, 99.2 % at 300 and at 3,000), charset-normalizer 3.5.2, installed in the round's scratch folder for the comparison, 2,784 (76.8 %: 64.6 %, 81.2 % and 85.1 %); on 3,616 samples of the applications' strings themselves, 97.3 % and 75.6 %. The confidence is a share among the readings, so a short text that several encodings read plausibly gets a low one: twelve bytes of Russian in windows-1251 are guessed right with 0.25

